2 TITL St Mary, Woodkirk
1 RIN ce0a1701-e0cc-4456-ac10-30fedca32665
1 _DSCR http://www.stmarywoodkirk.org/wp-content/uploads/2009/11/churchroofhole-300x225.jpg
2 CONT History of St Mary’s
2 CONT The building of St Mary’s dates from around 1100. The tower is original Norman, but the remainder of the church was rebuilt in the 1830s after a storm blew in the roof in 1832.
2 CONT The following is the text provided in the church’s grade II listed entry:
2 CONT SE22NE WF12 DEWSBURY ROAD SE272250 MORLEY (east side), Woodkirk 2/79 Church of St. Mary 7.8.64 GV II
2 CONT Church: Late C12 tower embattled c1911, nave rebuilt c1832 retaining inner medieval walls, chancel rebuilt and extended c1834 by Joseph Furness, a local mason. Large dressed stone to tower, hammer dressed stone to body of church with stone slate ro
2 CONC of. Gothic Revival style. West tower, nave, chancel, 3-stage embattled tower has battered plinth, roll-moulded strings. West face has lancet window to 1st stage, clock to 2nd stage and 2 light plate tracery belfry windows recessed inside elliptica
2 CONC l arches and having colonnettes with cushion-headed capitals. Battlements have corner pinnacles. Weathervane. Earlier steeply-pitched roofline visible on east-face. Nave has 3 bays of 2-light windows with trefoil heads and curvilinear tracery. Betw
2 CONC een 1st two bays gabled porch c1911. North facade of nave has 3 semicircular relieving arches, possibly from earlier church to aisle arcade now gone, blocked with 2-light traceried windows as front. Chancel set back from nave but roofed continuousl
2 CONC y has 5 bays. 1st-bay has 3-light window with intersecting Y-tracery. Other windows of 2 lights as nave. Priests’ door in 5th bay with small Y-traceried window above. On North facade of chancel, set between 1st and 2nd bay, priests’ door with point
2 CONC ed-arch and oak studded door. 5-light east window with intersecting Y-tracery. small added north vestry gabled and at right angles with chimney and coped gable.
2 CONT Interior: single vessel, the nave separated from chancel by semicircular arch. Oldest feature the tower arch: 2-centred with inner arch with chamfered octagonal piers with moulded capitals. Moulded plaster cornice, flat ceiling with rose c1830s. Ch
2 CONC ancel retains fine Jacobean pulpit of good work, octagonal with interlaced guilloche decoration and arcaded panels. Fine reused C15 bench ends to choir-stalls with carved rectilinear tracery and good carved poppy-heads. Vestry has walls panelled wi
2 CONC th remains of finely carved C17 box-pews. Window above Priests’ door made up of fragments of medieval stained glass. Other C19 stained glass memorial windows. Some memorials from earlier church, the best to Christopher Hodgson c1726. Royal Coat o
2 CONC f Arms (on canvas) to George I.
2 CONT Mentioned in Domesday Book c1086 and was granted by William, 2nd Earl Warenne, between 1121 and 1127 to the newly-forwed Priory of Augustinian Canons at Nostell. The Canons set up a small cell serving Woodkirk Church until the Dissolution in 1539 w
2 CONC hen it later fell into the hands of the Saviles. Excavations have revealed a courtyard over 50ft square with adjacent buildings. There was also a garden, an orchard and a stone dovecote with fishponds below the church which served as reservoirs fo
2 CONC r the watermills. St. Michael’s, East Ardsley (q.v.) was a chapel dependent on Woodkirk.
2 CONT Joan Thomas, St. Mary’s Woodkirk (undated church leaflet).
2 CONT 
2 CONT The following text is to be found in a frame in the church:
2 CONT Woodkirk means wooden church, originally a cell of Black Canons under Nostel Priory; it was formed in the reign of Henry I (1100-1135). Although only a cell to the Priory, it was of considerable extent, as was proved by the foundations of it extend
2 CONC ing over the gardens and Parsonage premises. In the valley beyond the east of the church the Canons had their fish ponds. The Church had an excellent water supply which was obtained from a well some 1.5-2 miles away, and piped in leaden pipes to th
2 CONC e church, the pipes being laid in the 15th century.
2 CONT A fair was held at Woodkirk, and during the reign of Edward II (1284-1327), it was reckoned amongst the most famous in the country, and to which it is said merchants from France, Spain, and even Germany came to sell their merchandise. During the ti
2 CONC me of the fair, the Priest and clerk stood ready all day, to marry all such as were desirous to enter the matrimonial state. Miracle plays were performed at the fair, the object to bring religion home in a tangible form to the frequenters. The fai
2 CONC r was later to become Lee Fair and lasted for three weeks and three days. At this present day only the first day (Lee Gap) and the last day (Latter Lee) are celebrated. The predominant industry of the area was “stone quarrying” followed by brick ma
2 CONC nufacturing and some coal mining.
2 CONT This text is found on a brass plaque framed in wood (shown in picture):
2 CONT May First 1722
2 CONT The Right Honourable George Earl of Cardigan Lord of the Manor of West Ardsley alias Woodchurch and three parts in four of the freeholders and others who have the right of Common ground therein have inclosed several parcells of the Waste or Commo
2 CONC n ground within the said Mannor containing by estimation fifty three Acres one rood or thereabouts and now in the several tenures or occupations of John Scott Whittaker Thomas Atack and William Awtice in Order to obtain the Bounty of the late Majes
2 CONC ty Queen Anne and also for a provision for the Minister of West Ardsley alias Woodchurch the better to encourage him to perform, Divine Offices in the said Church twice every Sunday viz to read prayers and preach forenoon and after
2 CONT This Inscription is put up at the change of the parish to perpetuate the memory of the said Benefaction
2 CONT In 1832, a great storm blew the roof in to the church and the walls and roof had to be rebuilt, as can be seen in this picture from one of the church windows.
2 CONT The ceiling and floor were replaced in 2007.
2 CONT The pipe organ was replaced with a Viscount digital organ in April 2010.
2 CONT St Mary’s contains the local war memorial, remembering those who died in the first and second world wars. Wakefield Family History has a detailed page about the men on the memorial. It can be found here.
2 CONT 
